detail.abstract:
This paper provides a comprehensive, multi-disciplinary examination of one of the most consequential governance questions of the early twenty-first century: who bears legal responsibility when an autonomous artificial intelligence agent operating in financial markets causes harm? The question is not merely academic. In February 2026, an AI-powered cryptocurrency trading bot called Lobstar Wilde emptied its entire $250,000 treasury in seconds due to a decimal-point programming error, demonstrating how autonomous agents can cause irreversible financial damage faster than any human oversight mechanism can respond. In July 2025, India's Securities and Exchange Board ordered Jane Street to place $565 million in escrow following findings that its algorithms had executed a coordinated pump-and-dump strategy in the Bank Nifty derivatives market over a sustained period. These are not isolated curiosities. They are symptoms of a structural accountability failure embedded in the design of modern financial markets.&nbsp; 
<div>
 The paper argues that autonomous financial agents — encompassing high-frequency trading algorithms, multi-agent DeFi liquidation bots, OpenClaw and PolyClaw-type architectures, and goal-directed AI portfolio managers — represent a qualitatively novel class of actor for which existing legal frameworks are structurally inadequate. The Roman actio de peculio addressed harms caused by slaves; respondeat superior addressed harms caused by employees; product liability addressed harms from defective goods. None of these doctrines, individually or in combination, adequately addresses the accountability vacuum created when an L4-level autonomous agent pursues objectives through pathways no human anticipated, in markets where the harm propagates faster than any human can intervene.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Through theoretical analysis spanning philosophy of agency, institutional economics, and systems theory; empirical examination of documented failures including the 2010 Flash Crash, Knight Capital's 2012 algorithm disaster, the 2022 Terra/LUNA stablecoin collapse, the FTX automated liquidation scandal, the 2019 Tyndaris v. Li litigation, and the 2025 Jane Street SEBI enforcement action; quantitative modelling of agent-attributable risk using decomposition frameworks and Monte Carlo simulation; and normative legal analysis spanning US, EU, UK, and African regulatory frameworks, this paper develops three original contributions to scholarship. First, it proposes a legally operative five-level, five-dimensional Autonomy Taxonomy calibrated to liability-relevant thresholds. Second, it develops a Graduated Liability Chain (GLC) model that allocates liability across the agent development and deployment ecosystem in proportion to autonomy contribution.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Third, it proposes the creation of a new legal category — the Algorithmic Legal Entity (ALE) — for fully autonomous L4 financial agents, creating liability-anchoring structures without attributing legal subjectivity to non-human actors.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper also addresses the foundational jurisprudential question: is 'Code the new Law,' or does law necessarily govern agents operating in financial markets? Drawing on Lawrence Lessig's regulatory theory, the crypto-anarchist Lex Cryptographia tradition, the failure of the Ethereum DAO hard-fork consensus, and Vili Lehdonvirta's analysis of digital platform governance, the paper argues that code-as-law is analytically incomplete, democratically illegitimate, and practically dangerous as a governance substitute. Law must and can govern autonomous agents through an Embedded Legality framework — encoding legal requirements as architectural mandates within agent design. The paper concludes with a phased international policy roadmap and identifies five priority empirical research programmes.
</div>
